00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:48.120
That is exactly why we are dedicating today's strategy spotlight to regime-based hedging.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:52.800
Regime-based hedging is an allocation approach that adjusts portfolio exposures

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.020
according to the prevailing macroeconomic regime rather than following static benchmarks.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.700
In a stag-flation environment of rising inflation combined with slowing growth,

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.340
traditional long-duration bonds and many equities become vulnerable.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.700
Certain real assets tend to outperform in those conditions.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.020
Today's conditions make this relevant because the closure of the Strait of Hormuz has

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.380
removed approximately 20% of global oil supply.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:21.400
This is accelerating core inflation and recreating the impossible trade the Fed faced

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.400
in the 1970s, raising rates would worsen unemployment and growth while cutting rates

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.900
would likely send inflation higher.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.900
Historical parallels show severe drawdowns with the S&amp;P 500 falling 48% in real terms from 1973

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:41.180
to 1975. Inflation reached 12.3% during that period and similar dynamics played out in 1978

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.040
to 1980. To implement this, you can screen for exchange traded funds with proven performance

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.680
in high inflation, low growth periods, focus on energy, broad commodities,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.740
and tips while avoiding long-duration fixed income.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.320
You should also avoid overexposure to private credit that cannot easily refinance at higher rates.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.800
Platforms like Wealth Simple or Interactive Brokers allow easy exchange traded fund rotation

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.600
with low commissions. Rebalance when inflation metrics or oil supply data shift the regime

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:18.180
probability. This strategy worked best in the 1970s inflationary decade but carries risks including

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:24.160
high volatility in commodities. There is also potential opportunity cost if inflation moderates

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.280
faster than expected. The key discipline is treating it as probabilistic regime allocation rather

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.580
than a permanent tilt. With these regime risks, front and center, we are putting capital to work in

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:41.000
a sector directly benefiting from the commodity dislocation. Here is today's practice

